sentences.t:
tres días e dos noches penssaron de andar,
alcançaron a mio Çid en Tévar e el pinar;
así viene[n] esforçado[s] que a manos se le cuyda[n] tomar.

sentences.l:
three days and two nights they-thought to go,
they-overtook my Çid in Tévar and the pine-wood;
so they-come strengthened that with hands him they-think to-take.

sentences.t:
Respuso el co[m]de: “esto non será verdad!
Lo de antes e de agora tódom lo pechará;
sabrá el salido a quien vino desondrar.”

sentences.l:
Replied the count: “this not will-be truth!
The of before and of now all-to-me it he-will-pay;
he-will-know the gone-out whom he-came to-dishonour.”

sentences.t:
Vi[di]eron la cuesta yuso la fuerça de los francos;
al fondón de la cuesta, çerca es de’llaño,
mandólos ferir mio Çid, el que en buen ora nasco:

sentences.l:
They-saw the slope down the force of the Franks;
at-the bottom of the slope, near is of-the-plain,
ordered-them to-strike my Çid, he who in a-good (hour) was-born:

sentences.t:
esto fazen los sos de voluntad e de grado;
los pendones e las lanças tan bien las van enpleando,
a los unos firiendo e a los otros derrocando.

sentences.l:
this do the his-men of will and of liking;
the pennons and the lances so well them they-go employing,
to the some striking and to the others throwing-down.

sentences.t:
I venció esta batalla por o ondró su barba,
prísole al co[m]de, pora su tie[nd]a lo levava;
a sos creenderos guardar lo mandava.

sentences.l:
There he-won this battle whereby he-honoured his beard,
took-him the count, to his tent him he-was-taking;
to his trusted-men to-guard him he-was-ordering.

sentences.t:
De fuera de la tienda un salto dava,
de todas partes los sos se ajunta[van];
plogo a mio Çid, ca grandes son las ganancias.

sentences.l:
From outside of the tent a leap he-was-giving,
from all parts the his-men themselves were-gathering;
it-pleased my Çid, for great are the gains.
